<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Errors in Division Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A laboratory technician prepares a saline solution by dissolving \({@mass@}\) g of salt into \({@volume@}\) ml of water. The mass is recorded correct to 1 decimal place and the volume correct to 2 decimal places. The concentration of the solution is calculated by dividing the mass by the volume. Find:</p>

<p>(a) The absolute error in the calculated concentration.</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>(b) The relative error in the concentration.</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>(c) The percentage error in the concentration.</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3> Worked Solution.</h3>
<p>The mass was recorded as \({@mass@}\) g, correct to 1 decimal place, and the volume as \({@volume@}\) ml, correct to 2 decimal places.</p>

<p><strong>Absolute errors in the individual measurements:</strong></p>
<p>Since the mass is correct to 1 decimal place, its absolute error is half of the smallest unit (0.1 g):</p>
<p>\[ \text{Mass error} = \frac{0.1}{2} = {@mass_err@} \text{ g} \]</p>
<p>Since the volume is correct to 2 decimal places, its absolute error is half of the smallest unit (0.01 ml):</p>
<p>\[ \text{Volume error} = \frac{0.01}{2} = {@volume_err@} \text{ ml} \]</p>

<p><strong>Important:</strong> When dividing two measured quantities, the <em>maximum</em> possible value of the result comes from pairing the <em>largest</em> possible numerator with the <em>smallest</em> possible denominator (since dividing by a smaller number gives a larger result). So:</p>
<p>\[ \text{Max mass} = {@mass@} + {@mass_err@} = {@max_mass@} \text{ g}, \qquad \text{Min volume} = {@volume@} - {@volume_err@} = {@min_volume@} \text{ ml} \]</p>

<p>(a) <strong>Absolute error in the concentration:</strong></p>
<p>Using the recorded measurements:</p>
<p>\[ \text{Working concentration} = \frac{{@mass@}}{{@volume@}} = {@conc@} \text{ g/ml} \]</p>
<p>Using the maximum mass and minimum volume:</p>
<p>\[ \text{Maximum concentration} = \frac{{@max_mass@}}{{@min_volume@}} = {@max_conc@} \text{ g/ml} \]</p>
<p>\[ \text{Absolute error} = \text{Maximum concentration} - \text{Working concentration} = {@max_conc@} - {@conc@} = {@ta1@} \text{ g/ml} \]</p>

<p>(b) <strong>Relative error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Relative error} = \frac{\text{Absolute error}}{\text{Working concentration}} = \frac{{@ta1@}}{{@conc@}} \approx {@ta2@} \]</p>

<p>(c) <strong>Percentage error:</strong></p>
<p>\[ \text{Percentage error} = \text{Relative error} \times 100\% = {@ta2@} \times 100\% \approx {@ta3@}\% \]</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
